00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.930
and how you can
benefit from that.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.790
What can you walk
away from this talk

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:10.832
in terms of tools and
infrastructure we have built,

00:01:10.832 --> 00:01:13.088
which you can start using
as soon as you step out

00:01:13.088 --> 00:01:13.630
of this room?

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:18.790
So let's get started.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.120
So before we jump
into things, what

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.710
do we mean by moving fast
and not breaking things?

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.240
Moving fast is good.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.400
It helps you innovate.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.795
It helps you respond to
critical issues and bugs sooner.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.290
It enables better productivity,
because to have a release

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.540
chain which keeps
going constantly,

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:50.630
you have to follow the
right engineering practices.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:55.190
And, lastly, it really makes
sure that code health is

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.740
prioritized for that particular
service or, in our case,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:03.620
the entire platform, because
you cannot move fast if you keep

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:04.630
building technical debt.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.550
But breaking things isn't.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.030
Why is that bad?

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.780
It's bad because it
erodes user satisfaction.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.330
Our customers who
use GCP are less

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:21.150
likely to recommend
GCP to other customers

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.740
if we constantly break them.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:26.670
It diminishes the brand.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.610
GCP is used by a bunch
of critical companies

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.580
and applications,
and we need to make

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:36.090
sure we have a high
uptime and a high quality.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.150
It makes it hard
for us internally,

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.960
as we have seen a few years back
when we started this journey,

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:46.120
to really land our features
and commitments to the roadmaps

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.250
we have committed
to our customers.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:52.760
And, lastly, quality
is table stakes when

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.330
it comes to a cloud platform.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:57.005
When working with
enterprise customers,

00:02:57.005 --> 00:03:00.860
quality is table stakes.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:05.450
So as an enterprise,
you need to move fast.

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:11.400
The expected time for
products has moved from--

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.610
shortened dramatically
from years

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:18.040
to days in a lot of cases.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.010
But, again, enterprise
customers want quality.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.100
To Mithra and me, a good
analogy we could think of

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.260
was transportation
and high-speed trains.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.150
You want to move fast.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:32.860
You want to get from
point A to point B

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.100
as quickly as possible,
but not while you

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:42.010
risk safety of the passengers.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:45.960
GCP is a set of
solutions and products

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.570
that helps our customers
solve the toughest business

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.700
challenges.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.860
We have external
paying customers

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.830
who run their applications
at a massive scale on GCP.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:05.820
Within Google and on GCP,
given its open source model,

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.520
we have a lot of developers
contributing to this platform

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.480
at all levels of the stack.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:16.300
These engineers want a
seamless development workflow

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:20.864
and want deterministic signals
through the workflow process.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:32.610
A quick overview-- what is
engineering productivity?

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.110
We started off as a test
engineering organization

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.190
which was focused mostly on
the part of the development

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.540
workflow after
code gets submitted

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:46.990
and how it gets validated,
deployed, and so on.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:52.100
About a decade back, we
started pivoting to engineering

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.800
productivity, and we started
looking at the entire software

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:02.070
development life
cycle holistically.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.590
We realized it's
important for us

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:07.130
to get involved right
from the design phase

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:11.070
and to also make sure that,
once our features, products,

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:14.390
and services are on production,
they actually are performing,

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:15.550
scaling.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.570
Within Google, we use
this term "landing."

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:18.650
Are they landing?

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.480
It's not good enough to launch.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:21.980
We want to make
sure things land.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:28.090
As Google started getting
involved in a lot more things,

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:32.050
we started getting involved
with applications within the ML

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:36.930
and AI space, back ends,
front ends, mobile.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.900
And we've been able to take
a lot of the learnings we've

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.660
had within Google over
the last two decades

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.410
and take the best parts
of it and actually

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:47.240
apply it to GCP and Anthos.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.390
So let's first ground
ourselves in some numbers

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.930
to give you all an idea of what
engineering productivity is

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:58.090
and how it fits into
GCP and within Google.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.350
As you can see on
the slide, Google

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:07.690
has about 2 million
lines of code and more--

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.970
60k code submissions,
800k builds,

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:15.080
150 million automated tests.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:20.270
More importantly, all of our
tools are built in-house,

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.310
but now we are starting to
build them in open source.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:25.370
Mithra will go into that.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:27.900
And they use the same
technologies as Google products

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:28.940
too.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:32.600
They run in the
same data centers.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.700
A SOA stack handles 2
million QPS, roughly.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:43.230
So the takeaway from this
slide is it's a massive scale.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.320
So what does EngProd
do as part of this?

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.800
Hopefully all of you
recognize what this is.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.030
If you're a GCP customer,
you probably use this.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:58.500
This is the cloud console.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.000
EngProd works very closely
with the Cloud Console team

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.830
to make sure we ship
this twice a week.

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:14.600
Our goal is to establish
biweekly high-quality release

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:16.055
guidance across GCP.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:20.960
The console is at the
higher part of the stack.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:22.985
It makes sense to
ship it twice a week.

00:07:22.985 --> 00:07:27.370
Our lower-risk storage systems,
code infrastructure systems,

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:30.020
we don't want to ship
them twice a week.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.470
But we do want to ship
them with regular cadence

00:07:32.470 --> 00:07:36.340
for the reasons I
explained before.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.710
But we strongly believe
that, internally, we

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:43.730
want to have as frequent
healthy snapshots in most cases,

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:47.290
like daily release
candidates, as we call it.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:51.980
It gives us the ability to
go faster if we need to.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:56.060
So how are we ensuring this,
balancing developer velocity

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:56.660
and quality?

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:04.240
We have created a set of guiding
principles that let us balance

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:08.930
developer velocity and quality.

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:11.180
We want to share these
practices and tools with you

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:16.080
all so you can accelerate
at the same pace as we do.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.440
We deliberated on a
philosophy that we just

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:28.470
don't want to be
comfortable catching bugs.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.210
We want to prevent them.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:35.950
This meant that, in addition
to doing complex qualification

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:39.789
across systems, we
started building tooling

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:43.390
and infrastructure that
enables developers themselves

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:47.880
to qualify their code
before they submit it,

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:52.260
going back to the notion
of preventing things.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.460
This changed the way we
built, qualified, deployed,

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.950
and monitored our
applications, again

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:05.193
to balance developer
velocity and quality.

00:09:05.193 --> 00:09:07.110
Let's look at what our
guiding principles are.

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:14.830
Our guiding principles
are driving test health,

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:18.450
avoiding performance
regressions,

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:22.590
ensuring high-quality releases.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:26.170
All of it is anchored in
insightful and actionable

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:26.670
metrics.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:33.590
Let's take a closer look
into these principles

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:34.882
and see what I'm talking about.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:45.720
With test health, we aim for
hermetic integration tests,

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:49.980
fast pre-submits, and
elimination of test flakiness.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:55.530
Internally, we have built
a cloud integration testing

00:09:55.530 --> 00:10:01.630
framework that incorporates
these learnings and principles

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:06.780
and enables developers to
write tests once and run them

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:09.160
anywhere.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:10.480
I'm going to repeat that--

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:15.790
write tests once,
run them anywhere.

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:20.650
That is important because
that has allowed us to really

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:24.340
amplify the value of our tests.

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.530
And because the
tests are hermetic,

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:30.790
they are fast, they
don't have flakiness,

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:32.500
and that has really
boosted our ability

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:35.290
to increase the amount
of validation we

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:38.950
can do for a feature,
right from the time

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:41.740
it starts getting
developed to even how it

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.560
performs in production.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.700
Under the hood, we have
a bunch of technologies

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.610
like resource
pre-provisioning, record replay

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:54.892
to speed up setup and tear down,
which, for those of you who

00:10:54.892 --> 00:10:57.100
are in this space, know it
takes up a lot of the time

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:58.558
when it comes to
integration tests.

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:05.770
So let's look at our
next guiding principle--

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:07.692
avoiding performance
regressions.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:13.770
We want to make performance
a first-class citizen.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:20.160
We have done that by building
a performance benchmarking

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.530
library internally that
service owners and products can

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:28.230
seamlessly integrate
into their products

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:29.940
and get rich
performance benchmarks.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:37.460
This is now a standard, not only
within GCP but within Google.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:42.740
This in turn has allowed us
to keep monitoring performance

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:46.460
as a feature or a service
keeps getting developed and not

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:48.500
as an afterthought once
we put it on production

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:51.110
and then figure it out, oh, it
doesn't scale, or it's slow.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:58.730
Lastly, our third principal--

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:00.110
ensuring high-quality releases.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:05.660
We wanted to take this
slide as an opportunity

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:10.310
to say that there's a balance
between tooling and process.

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:12.650
As much as effective
tooling is important--

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:17.140
and we have built a bunch of
great tooling in this space--

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:19.630
I think we have learned that
effective process is important

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:23.290
when it comes to
high-quality releases.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:28.490
We have distilled
the problem on GCP

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:30.560
that if the rate
at which we push

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:33.860
our features from
development to production,

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:37.085
if you're doing that well, we're
ensuring high-quality releases.

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:42.510
We have learned a bunch
of best practices.

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:46.210
We wanted to share a few
of them with you all here.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:50.210
One of the most important
things we realized

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:55.310
is if you decouple the features
and the binaries, that really

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:57.050
helps your binaries
and your releases

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:00.660
to be a release gain, one
that goes out every day,

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:04.320
every week depending
on the service.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:08.960
And if there's a certain problem
that shows up in production,

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:11.290
it's mostly guarded
behind a feature flag.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:14.210
You can just turn off the
flag, and the releases

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:14.890
keep going out.

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:20.860
This also helps
us really install

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:24.880
the engineering discipline we
need for the last bullet here.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:28.630
If that is a problem introduced
not by a particular feature,

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:33.840
there is some global
state in the architecture,

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.670
we have the culture
to figure out

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:41.160
what caused the problem, debug
it, find our exact change,

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:46.170
revert it, cherry-pick the
change that caused the problem,

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.770
let the chain keep going,
and go fix it and put it

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:50.520
in the next chain.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:56.450
This ensures that we
are not taking shortcuts

00:13:56.450 --> 00:14:00.280
on critical fixes, critical
P0's, and so on and so forth.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:07.860
Most importantly, we measure
all of these biometrics.

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.580
I read this somewhere
literally half an hour back.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:18.200
I thought it was good
to share it here.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.490
If you don't know
where you come from,

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:22.460
you don't know where you are.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:24.380
If you don't know
where you are, you

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:27.070
don't know where you're going.

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:28.660
If you don't know
where you're going,

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:30.291
you're probably going
somewhere wrong.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:37.591
That brings me to
project health.

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:45.270
We balance developer
velocity and quality

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:50.210
at Google and on GCP via
Project Health, which

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:51.430
we refer to as pH.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:58.600
We track key metrics across
developer productivity,

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:03.075
release velocity, reliability,
and, most importantly, quality.

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:10.240
We automatically calculate
a project health level, a pH

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:15.970
level, based on
all these metrics.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:20.500
We have years of data
at Google and within GCP

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:24.280
that the higher a project's
project health level is,

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:30.420
the better they do at balancing
developer velocity and quality.

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:33.850
A higher pH means better
development practices.

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:38.300
A higher pH means a better
ability to fix issues.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:42.440
A higher pH means you're
following your focus.

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:46.490
You can focus on coding
and not pushing releases

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:47.990
because they are
automatically going

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:51.360
on, and so on and so forth.

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:55.430
Let me ground this with
a concrete example.

00:15:55.430 --> 00:15:57.630
Here's an example of a
project health dashboard

00:15:57.630 --> 00:15:59.080
for a particular area in GCP.

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:04.640
This is the view that
service area gets, saying

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:05.920
these are all your projects.

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:09.210
Here's your overall
project health level.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:14.880
This gives them an ability to
zoom into one of these areas

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.700
and figure out which
one of these metrics

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:19.950
they are struggling
in and what they need

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:23.540
to devote their resources on.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:26.630
As engineering
productivity, this is also

00:16:26.630 --> 00:16:29.788
very useful information for us.

00:16:29.788 --> 00:16:31.330
Because, very often,
there a lot more

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:37.270
developers working on a service
than productivity engineers

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:39.470
working with that service.

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:41.570
So this helps us
prioritize our efforts

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:43.720
and figure out what
are the hot spots

00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:47.800
for this particular product
or area and zoom in on that.

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:57.120
So recapping, our
guiding principles

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:00.900
are driving test health,
avoiding performance

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:04.560
regressions, make performance
a first-class citizen, not

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:08.770
an afterthought, ensure
high-quality releases,

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:12.550
both by effective tooling
and process and engineering

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:18.040
discipline, and
measure everything.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:21.520
I'll invite Mithra
now to talk about what

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:23.290
we are doing along
these principles

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:27.700
when it comes to Anthos,
the tools we have built

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:29.530
and how you all can
benefit from them.

00:17:32.904 --> 00:17:37.740
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:40.470
MITHRA RAJAH: Hi, everyone.

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:45.300
The outside world now uses
VMs, Kubernetes, GitHub,

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:46.730
and containers.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.630
Google software
traditionally used

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:51.270
to be stored in a single
shared repository.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:54.060
But our landscape is
continuously changing

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:56.550
and we need to evolve to
adapt to this changing

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:59.550
landscape as we are
building tooling for Anthos

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:02.600
and further on.

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:04.900
So let's look at open
source development.

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:07.910
Google works very closely
with Kubernetes and Istio

00:18:07.910 --> 00:18:10.930
open source communities
to develop a lot of tools

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:14.560
to improve development
velocity as well as quality.

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:18.820
We contribute very actively
to SIG testing in Kubernetes.

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:22.330
Let us dig a little bit deeper
into the test infrastructure

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:24.100
along the same
guiding principles

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:25.990
that you saw Ankit
talk about just now.

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:30.550
Driving test health.

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:33.250
In the open source
community for Kubernetes,

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:36.660
we helped build the continuous
integration system called Prow.

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:41.080
Prow started in 2016, and we
replaced Jenkins as the system

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:42.580
for Kubernetes.

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:43.790
Why is this important?

00:18:43.790 --> 00:18:47.260
Because we no longer need
to run a Jenkins master,

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:50.830
and we can update the service
without any down time.

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:52.660
Another important
thing is that we

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:55.090
test Kubernetes on Kubernetes.

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:57.400
It is very easy to
maintain and deploy

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:02.800
and respond to GitHub webhooks
rather than using a munger.

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:03.610
Testgrid.

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:06.220
So Testgrid provides a
grid of test results,

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:09.410
which gives you very fast
access to test results.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:11.350
You can also highly
configure this

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.710
to look at all the bug
data, and sort through data,

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:18.520
and get like data about grouping
or filing bugs, and so on.

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:20.620
This is integrated
with cloud build.

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:24.380
And so you can go to Results
Store to view all of the cloud

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:29.310
build results and logs and
do more analysis on the data.

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:31.070
Let us see what we
have done in the space

00:19:31.070 --> 00:19:35.510
of hermetic infrastructure
when it comes to open source.

00:19:35.510 --> 00:19:38.540
Some of you may be familiar
with Kubernetes and Docker.

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:41.180
This is another open source
tool available for use

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:43.016
to you in Kubernetes.

00:19:43.016 --> 00:19:47.150
This provides hermetic testing
via a local Kubernetes cluster

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:49.850
that you can run on your
development workstation.

00:19:49.850 --> 00:19:51.870
There are no
external dependencies

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:53.840
and it helps you
integration test Kubernetes

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:58.620
applications with other things,
like Istio or Nomos, and so on.

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:02.500
Let's see a quick demo of
how KIND works in action.

00:20:02.500 --> 00:20:04.220
I'll give you a
second to absorb this.

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:17.520
Yeah, as you can
see, the cluster

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:19.800
gets created really quickly.

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:22.650
And, basically, it creates
an entire Kubernetes cluster

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:25.050
in a matter of seconds
when you compare that

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:27.350
to a cluster on cloud,
which takes about 4

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:30.550
to 5 minutes to start up.

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:33.120
Avoiding performance
regressions.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:35.910
We want to make performance a
first-class citizen, like Ankit

00:20:35.910 --> 00:20:37.690
was just talking about.

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:39.450
We want to provide
you the ability

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:42.600
to visualize performance data
and catch regressions really

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:43.650
quickly.

00:20:43.650 --> 00:20:46.170
And so we are working on
open sourcing tooling that

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:48.330
lets you visualize
performance data

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:52.320
and find these regressions,
just as we do for GCP.

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:55.740
This graph shows the trend
for a performance benchmark.

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:58.980
And you can see that there is
a developer checking in a fix.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:02.070
And then that caused the
performance regression

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:05.810
to be fixed, and then the
curve came down again.

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:09.090
And this kind of lets developers
collaborate with each other

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:11.400
around performance.

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:13.750
We've already deployed this
to some of our customers

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:15.750
and we are working to
bring this to open source.

00:21:18.410 --> 00:21:20.360
Talking about
high-quality releases,

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:24.740
I wanted to highlight how
Jenkins X uses Prow for CI/CD

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:27.230
alongside the Knative
build pipelines.

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:29.990
Let's walk through the
process a little bit.

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:32.960
A developer creates a PR.

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:37.250
GitHub then sends the
PR notification to Prow.

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:40.520
Prow creates a build
on Knative, and it

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:43.310
runs the jenkinsfile
pipeline, and the results

00:21:43.310 --> 00:21:45.330
get uploaded resultstore.

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:46.880
So this is one of
the ways that you

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:50.960
can automate your full CI/CD
pipeline using Prow and Jenkins

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:52.910
X on open source.

00:21:55.670 --> 00:21:58.510
And let's take a look
at actionable metrics.

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:00.850
Kubernetes was
essentially designed

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:05.890
for software portability and
like developer portability.

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:08.470
To kind of ensure
consistency and portability

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:11.530
when we move across cloud
providers and cloud,

00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:14.470
we worked with Kubernetes
for the CNCF, which

00:22:14.470 --> 00:22:17.320
is Cloud Native Compute
Foundation, as you might know,

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:21.430
and helped design or implement
the conformance program.

00:22:21.430 --> 00:22:25.210
This infrastructure lets you
measure the end-to-end coverage

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:29.140
when it comes to Kubernetes
APIs and visualize the results

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:30.880
of coverage over time.

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:32.680
This is all integrated
into Testgrid,

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:36.670
so you can view the coverage
numbers for Kubernetes APIs.

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:39.010
We've also enabled
this for Knative.

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:42.040
And so, here, you can see
a graph that shows you

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:44.330
how the coverage
numbers trend over time,

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:48.090
and you can see the
results on Testgrid.

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:52.440
And then, moving on to a
hybrid cloud model with Anthos.

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:55.470
We wanted to make sure that,
as the landscape evolves

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:57.990
to support hybrid
cloud and multi-cloud,

00:22:57.990 --> 00:23:02.460
we used the same CI/CD and
tooling processes to also test

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:04.290
a hybrid cloud platform.

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:08.430
So we make use of the
same CI and CD systems

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:10.910
with Prow,
resultstore, and so on

00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:15.090
and make sure that we can also
test on-prem the same way we

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:16.020
test on GCP.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:20.540
Let's take a little
bit of a closer

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:24.370
look at the qualification
pipeline for Anthos.

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:27.960
So a developer has
code in his repository,

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:29.810
and this is the
system under test.

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:31.610
So we want to make
sure that you are

00:23:31.610 --> 00:23:35.760
testing your pre-submit flows
and submitting the code.

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:39.140
When code gets submitted,
a git checkout happens,

00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:43.280
and we run an on-prem cluster
of Prow on a vSphere lab.

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:46.370
And then this creates a GKE
cluster, runs all the tests,

00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:50.540
and then publishes the
test results to Testgrid.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:52.600
Once the developer
submits the code,

00:23:52.600 --> 00:23:54.550
we also run tests
on post-submit.

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:56.080
Certain tests are
run pre-submit,

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:57.910
and some on post-submit.

00:23:57.910 --> 00:24:01.270
And you can basically see
how Prow again runs the tests

00:24:01.270 --> 00:24:04.630
and publishes the
results to Testgrid.

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:08.470
So this helps us really
easily interpret the CI signal

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:11.880
towards release readiness.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:15.360
So to summarize again,
engineering productivity

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:19.050
makes quality software
development easier and faster.

00:24:19.050 --> 00:24:21.300
And we do this by
adhering to our guiding

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:23.780
principles of
driving test health,

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:26.130
avoiding performance
regression, and ensuring

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:28.210
high-quality releases.

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:31.350
And you saw a little bit about
Anthos engineering productivity

00:24:31.350 --> 00:24:34.080
tools and how a lot of them
are available for your use

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:35.160
and benefit.

00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:39.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

